{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Capability call sites are cast, not guarded — a non-capable transport fails silently\n\n## The gap\n\nTicket `cc77-8120-bcc6-47e8` annotated the core reconciler's `client`/`transport` parameters with\nthe `TicketTransport` port. Four call sites then reported `[attr-defined]` because they reach for\nOPT-IN capability members that the base port deliberately does not declare:\n\n| site (re-located 2026-08-11) | member |\n|---|---|\n| `dispatch_one.py:417` (`create_one`) | `add_comment` |\n| `dispatch_one.py:657` (`_update_one_scalar_update`) | `add_comment` |\n| `dispatch_one.py:742` (`_update_one_dispatch_links`) | `set_relationship` |\n| `dispatch_apply_phases.py:165` (`_update_one_dispatch_comments`) | `add_comment` |\n\nEach was correctly resolved with `cast(\"SupportsComments\", client)` / `cast(\"SupportsLinks\", client)`\n— correct, because widening to `Any` would have destroyed the enforcement cc77 exists to add.\n\n**But a `cast` is a static assertion with NO runtime effect.** None of the four sites checks that the\ntransport actually HAS the member. Compare `fetcher.py:597/647/688`, which gate the same class of\noptional capability on `hasattr` before calling. The reconciler is inconsistent with itself.\n\n## Why the failure would be silent\n\nAn `AttributeError` at any of these sites lands inside a broad `except Exception` on the dispatch\npath, so a transport missing an opt-in capability would not crash the pass and would not surface a\nbridge alert — the mutation is never applied and the pass reports success. That conflates\n\"capability absent (designed, fine to skip)\" with \"capability present but threw (a real failure)\".\nSame silent-soft-failure shape as `d067-5d2f-bcee-46ae`. It matters now because the whole point of\nthe capability Protocols is that a SECOND backend (Data Center) may legitimately not implement them.\n\n## OPERATOR DECISION (2026-08-12, on record)\n\nImplement the runtime guards AND report skip-vs-failure **distinctly through the EXISTING bridge\naudit/log channel**. Do **not** invent a new alert mechanism.\n\n- Guard shape: `isinstance(client, SupportsComments)` / `isinstance(client, SupportsLinks)` —\n  ADR-0083:193-198 prescribes `isinstance`-guarded capability detection against the\n  `@runtime_checkable` capability Protocols in `_backend.py`, and both are already\n  `@runtime_checkable`. This is a deliberate, documented divergence from `fetcher.py`'s `hasattr`\n  (which predates the Protocols and probes single members, not the capability); the divergence is\n  justified in an in-code comment per AC3.\n- Capability **absent** → explicit designed skip: an INFO log naming the missing capability and the\n  site, the sub-op still **counted** as computed (so it is queryable, never a silent zero), and a\n  durable record on the EXISTING `bridge_alerts` channel (`pass_io._load_alert_store()`, the same\n  store `record_parent_divergence` and `_record_reporter_alert` already write) under a **distinct**\n  kind: `outbound-comment-capability-absent` / `outbound-link-capability-absent`.\n- Capability **present but raised** → unchanged existing failure path: `logger.warning` /\n  stderr with the real exception surfaced, plus in-band `comment_errors` capture where that already\n  exists. Never swallowed silently as before.\n- `cast(...)` is NOT replaced by `Any` anywhere.\n\n### Module-size constraint (drives one structural move)\n\n`dispatch_one.py` is 796/800 LOC — 4 lines of headroom, and the CI module-size gate\n(`.github/module-size-limit.txt`, `_build-and-test.yml`) is absolute. Even the leanest guard\nform (~11 added lines) overflows it, so room must be made.\n\nThe room is made by relocating **`_call_with_retry`** (`dispatch_one.py:71-145`, ~75 lines) into\n`dispatch_apply_phases.py`. This is an EXISTING one-directional seam — `dispatch_one` already\nimports five names from `dispatch_apply_phases` (`_find_link_id`, `_index_existing_links`,\n`_update_one_apply_reporter`, `_update_one_dispatch_comments`, `_update_one_filter_fields`), so\nmoving a shared primitive DOWN that edge introduces no cycle, and it lets the guarded\n`set_relationship` retry keep working from either module. `dispatch_one` re-exports the name, so\nthe attribute refs `dispatch_one._call_with_retry` (test_cloud_rate_limit_sweep_heldout.py:269,\ntest_timeout_s_forwarding_heldout_7504.py:22) and `batch_dispatch._call_with_retry`\n(test_error_taxonomy.py:68) keep resolving, as do `applier`/`apply_outbound`'s imports.\nPost-move: dispatch_one ≈ 730, dispatch_apply_phases ≈ 335 — both inside the 200-500 band.\n\n**Deliberately NOT moved:** `_update_one_dispatch_links` and `create_one` are the only two\ndispatch_one functions carrying `.github/complexity-baseline.json` entries\n(lines 25-26: `dispatch_one.py::_update_one_dispatch_links` = 17,\n`dispatch_one.py::create_one` = 36). That file is **shrink-only and LOCKED against main** — the\nCI \"Complexity-baseline lock\" step (`check_complexity_baseline.py --lock`) requires an\nADMINISTRATOR override for an added entry, and re-keying a moved function to a new module path\nIS an added entry. Relocating either would therefore need an admin force-submit. `_call_with_retry`\ncarries no baseline entry (verified: absent from the file), so moving it re-keys nothing, adds no\nentry, and needs no override. AC8 asserts this.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 — all four capability call sites are runtime-guarded with `isinstance` against the\n      relevant `@runtime_checkable` capability Protocol before the call:\n      `create_one` (add_comment), `_update_one_scalar_update` comment-fallback (add_comment),\n      `_update_one_dispatch_links` (set_relationship), `_update_one_dispatch_comments` (add_comment).\n- [ ] AC2 — capability ABSENT is a designed skip that is OBSERVABLE, asserted by a unit test with\n      a transport implementing neither `SupportsComments` nor `SupportsLinks`. The observable\n      signal differs per site because the sites are heterogeneous, and the AC is per-site:\n      * ALL FOUR sites: an INFO log naming the missing capability and the site, AND a\n        `bridge_alerts` record whose `kind` is the capability-absent kind.\n      * `_update_one_dispatch_comments` and `_update_one_dispatch_links` ONLY (the two phase\n        helpers that already `return (computed, applied)`): additionally a non-zero `*_computed`\n        with `*_applied == 0`, so the existing silent-no-op canary in `apply_handlers.py` also\n        fires. `create_one` and the `_update_one_scalar_update` fallback have NO local\n        computed/applied counter and none is added — the log + alert are their whole signal.\n      * No `AttributeError` is raised, and none is swallowed.\n- [ ] AC3 — capability PRESENT but raising is distinct, asserted by a unit test: a transport that\n      HAS `add_comment`/`set_relationship` but raises produces the pre-existing failure signal\n      (warning/stderr with the real exception; a `comment_errors` entry on the comment paths) and\n      does NOT produce a capability-absent alert kind. Conversely a capability-absent skip does NOT\n      append to `comment_errors` — that channel stays the failure channel. The two are never conflated.\n- [ ] AC4 — the divergence from `fetcher.py`'s `hasattr` shape is justified in an in-code comment\n      citing ADR-0083.\n- [ ] AC5 — [operator-attested] mutation-checked: deleting the guard at a site turns the AC2 test\n      RED with a message naming the missing capability (the alert-kind assertion), not an incidental\n      `AttributeError`; verdict recorded as a comment here.\n- [ ] AC6 — `cast(...)` is NOT removed in favour of `Any` anywhere; `make typecheck` stays green.\n- [ ] AC7 — no `src/rebar` file exceeds the 800-LOC module-size cap after the change\n      (`dispatch_one.py` and `dispatch_apply_phases.py` re-measured with `wc -l`).\n- [ ] AC8 — `.github/complexity-baseline.json` is UNCHANGED by this ticket: no entry added, none\n      raised, no key re-pathed. Verified by `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check`\n      (run inside `make lint`) plus `git diff --exit-code .github/complexity-baseline.json`.\n- [ ] AC9 — the `_call_with_retry` relocation is behaviour-preserving: the existing retry tests\n      (`test_retry_httperror.py`, `test_error_taxonomy.py`, `test_cloud_rate_limit_sweep_heldout.py`,\n      `test_timeout_s_forwarding_heldout_7504.py`) pass UNMODIFIED against the re-export.\n\n## Verify\n\n- `make lint`\n- `make typecheck`\n- `python -m pytest tests/unit -q -k \"dispatch or reconciler\"`\n- `python -m pytest tests/interfaces -q -k \"reconcile\"`\n- `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` (also inside `make lint`)\n- `git diff --exit-code .github/complexity-baseline.json`\n- `wc -l src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_one.py src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_apply_phases.py`"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786505744887616001,"uuid":"61473b50-15d3-4063-8be8-f86ce1800e46"}